</div>

<p>In general, the grace period for registering a vehicle starts when you first enter the new state. If you decide to register your vehicle during this grace period, follow these steps:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Visit the RTO office in your new state.</li>
  <li>Submit the documents mentioned above.</li>
  <li>Complete the necessary paperwork and obtain the new registration and insurance documents.</li>
  <li>Pay the required fees for vehicle registration and insurance.</li>
</ol>

<p>Be aware that the grace period does not apply if you register your vehicle after the grace period expires or if you have already used your vehicle in the new state for more than 15 days.</p>

<p>When registering your vehicle in your new state, you may need to pay road taxes based on the vehicle's mileage, age, and engine capacity. Some states may also require additional taxes due to the vehicle's weight or emissions. To calculate the road tax due, you'll need to know the following details:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Mileage:</strong> Obtain the vehicle's mileage from the odometer reading mentioned in the vehicle's registration certificate or insurance papers.</li>
  <li><strong>Engine capacity:</strong> Check the engine capacity from the vehicle's registration certificate or manufacturer's documentation.</li>
  <li><strong>Age:</strong> Obtain the vehicle's age from the registration certificate or insurance papers, which is usually mentioned as the date the vehicle was first registered.</li>
</ul>

<p>To calculate the road tax, you can follow these steps:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Check the vehicle's mileage and engine capacity.</li>
  <li>Visit the RTO office in your new state, providing the required details (mileage, engine capacity, age).</li>
  <li>Request the RTO to calculate the road tax based on the provided information.</li>
  <li>Pay the calculated road tax, which may include additional taxes based on vehicle weight or emissions.</li>
  <li>Receive the new registration certificate and insurance papers for the vehicle.</li>
</ol></div>
